# Let the Bluetooth HAL open its HCI socket on a 4.9 kernel.
#
# The AIDL default HAL talks to the kernel over AF_BLUETOOTH:
# net_bluetooth_mgmt.cpp does socket(PF_BLUETOOTH, SOCK_RAW, BTPROTO_HCI),
# bind, read, write. porg selects it (TARGET_TEGRA_BT := btlinux), and the
# .rc already grants NET_ADMIN, so nothing else is missing.
#
# AOSP's own policy grants it as self:bluetooth_socket -- a class that only
# exists from Linux 4.13, where commit da69a5306ab9 gave every address family
# its own security class. This kernel is 4.9, so its classmap has no entry for
# AF_BLUETOOTH and the access arrives as the generic self:socket instead:
#
#   avc: denied { create } for comm="android.hardwar" tclass=socket
#        scontext=u:r:hal_bluetooth_default:s0
#
# so the rule upstream wrote is simply never consulted and the HAL cannot
# start. hal_bluetooth_btlinux -- the other HAL that uses the same transport --
# carries *both* lines for exactly this reason; hal_bluetooth_default was only
# ever given the new one. Same permissions as btlinux, no more.
#
# This belongs in AOSP's system/sepolicy/vendor/hal_bluetooth_default.te.
allow hal_bluetooth_default self:socket { create bind read write };
